member.summary:
Issue #4961 / ADR-0201: binds the header shared by the if let STATEMENT and EXPRESSION — the bindings, the optional guard, and the then-region — with one scope and one narrowing frame, so both forms make the same scoping decisions:
every bound name is declared in a fresh scope that covers later
initializers, the guard, and the then-region, and is popped before the caller binds the else branch (bindings are never visible there or after the form);
each binding is added to the narrowing frame as soon as it binds, so a
later initializer, the guard, and the then-region all observe earlier bindings at their non-null underlying types;
the then-region sees the guard like an if branch sees its
condition: the narrowing a true guard implies and its when-true pattern variables;
when a guard is present and the final initializer — which the shared
parser ended at the guard's && — is a non-nullable bool, GS0624 replaces GS0296. A nullable conversion hiding that boolean in an assignment/conditional continuation gets the same migration hint.
The caller still owns the lowering, which differs only because one form is a statement and the other produces a value.

ADR-0071 / issue #708, issue #4961 / ADR-0201: binds the if let STATEMENT. The header (bindings, optional guard, then-region) goes through , shared with the expression form, so a later initializer and the guard see earlier bindings narrowed and no binding is visible in the else branch. The lowering short-circuits left to right. Without an else branch (or with a single test) it stays in nested statements:if let a = e0, let b = e1 && g { T }
            =>
            { let a = e0; if a != nil { let b = e1; if b != nil && g { T } } }
With an else branch and more than one test it lowers to forward conditional gotos to a single else label, so each branch appears exactly once and definite-assignment and return analysis see plain control flow.

member.remarks:
The emitted shape is:let ch_i = <operand>                 // once, left to right
            var winner = -1; var again = true
            loop:
            let w = SelectWaiter.Rent(n, ctx)
            try {
            w.AddReceive[T](ch_i, i) / w.AddSend[T](ch_i, v_i, i)
            winner = w.Wait()                 // or w.TryNow() when a default arm exists
            again = w.NeedsReprobe            // a foreign arm lost its value to a thief
            if !again { v_i = w.TakeValue[T]() }
            } finally { w.Return() }
            if again goto loop
            if winner == i { body_i } … else { default body }
Wait is the blocking form; inside a state machine the async lowering turns it into an awaited WaitAsync, exactly as it does for a scope's join, so a parked select holds no thread.

member.summary:
Gets this node's immediate child statements, expressions and patterns, in the order visits them — the Roslyn IOperation.ChildOperations analogue (ADR-0169, issue #4436). Helper nodes that are not themselves statements, expressions or patterns (a switch arm, a catch clause) are transparent: their own children are listed in their place. Three shapes follow Roslyn's operation tree rather than the compiler walker:
a function literal's child is its body, as an IAnonymousFunctionOperation's is (the walker
treats the body as a separate scope and does not enter it);
an assignment's children are its target, then its value;
a plain x is T has only its operand as a child, as an IIsTypeOperation does; the type pattern
G# binds under it is not listed.

Fallback walker for expression/statement kinds the linear simulation has no explicit case for (channel receive/send/close, index and member access, interpolated strings, …). It routes nested flow-sensitive block and branch expressions back through and:
recursively analyzes nested function literals against the assignment state at their capture point
(the C# model: a use of a captured local inside the literal is an error unless the local was definitely assigned before the literal, or is assigned inside it);
reports GS0522 for value reads of tracked no-zero-value locals (issue #3316);
skips &v subtrees: an address-of operand is a ref/out argument position, not a value read —
unassigned ref-reads are GS0239/GS9003 territory, and reporting them here would double-report against the ref-kind checks.

member.remarks:
The CLR does not allow suspension inside a catch or finally handler. This pass lifts the handler body out of the protected region by capturing exceptions into a pending-exception local and executing the original handler body after the try statement completes.
<b>Pattern A — try/catch with await in catch:</b>Exception pendingException = null;
            T? capture_i = null;                 // per-clause capture of original type
            try { body; }
            catch (T e) { capture_i = e; pendingException = e; }   // KEEP ORIGINAL TYPE
            if (capture_i != null) {
            T e = capture_i;                 // rebind original variable
            handlerBody;                     // await is now outside the handler
            }
Keeping the catch typed (instead of widening to Exception) is essential for correctness: it ensures the CLR catch dispatch only fires for the original handler's exception type, so subsequent catch arms — and the rethrow paths for unmatched exceptions — keep their original semantics (issue #419).
<b>Pattern B — try/finally with await in finally:</b>Exception pendingException = null;
            try { body; }
            catch (Exception ex) { pendingException = ex; }
            finallyBody;   // await is now outside the finally region
            if (pendingException != null) {
            System.Runtime.ExceptionServices.ExceptionDispatchInfo
            .Capture(pendingException).Throw();   // preserve original stack trace (#418)
            }
<b>Pattern C — non-fall-through exits out of a try whose finally awaits (issue #1484):</b> Every way control leaves a try whose finally awaits must run the finally <i>after</i> the awaited work completes and only then resume that exit. The exception exit is already generalized by Pattern B; Pattern C generalizes the remaining non-fall-through exits — return and the intra-method branches (goto/break/continue, all of which reach this pass as / after the general  desugars loops) — that target a label outside the protected region (or, for return, leave the method). Mirroring the pending-exception machinery, each such exit is replaced by an assignment to a small pendingBranch discriminator (plus a pendingReturnValue local for value returns, captured at the point of the original return so evaluation order is preserved) followed by a goto to the lifted-finally tail. After the finally body and the pending-exception rethrow, a dispatch on the discriminator resumes the captured exit:int pendingBranch = 0;          // 0 == fall-through
            T pendingReturnValue = default; // only when a value-return is captured
            try { body; /* `return v;` => { pendingReturnValue = v; pendingBranch = 1; goto finallyTail; } */ }
            catch (Exception ex) { pendingException = ex; }
            finallyTail:
            finallyBody;                    // await is now outside the finally region
            if (pendingException != null) { ...Capture(pendingException).Throw(); }
            if (pendingBranch == 1) { return pendingReturnValue; }  // resume captured return
            if (pendingBranch == 2) { goto someOuterLabel; }        // resume captured goto/break/continue
            // pendingBranch == 0 falls through and continues normally
The rewrite is compositional: an exit that crosses multiple awaited finallys is captured one level at a time, because each level's dispatch re-emits a real return/goto that the next enclosing lifted try captures in turn. The discriminator and pending-return-value locals are ordinary user-style locals, so  hoists them into state-machine fields exactly like the pending-exception local, keeping them live across the awaited finally.
